                <span class="topic-pill">ItTechGenie</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <div class="row g-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Friendly Introduction</h2>
                <p>When people hear <em>microservices</em>, they often jump straight into distributed systems. But in real projects, that is rarely how things start. Most products begin as a monolith, become layered for maintainability, and only then move toward distributed services when business pressure demands it.</p>
                <p class="mb-0">This lesson gives you a practical roadmap using an e-commerce domain, so you can understand not only <strong>what</strong> each architecture is, but also <strong>when</strong> and <strong>why</strong> to use it.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">In this lesson, we will discuss</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li>What monolithic, layered, and distributed systems really mean.</li>
                    <li>How an e-commerce app evolves from one style to another.</li>
                    <li>Strengths, trade-offs, and practical transition guidance.</li>
                    <li>Best practices and mistakes teams make during migration.</li>
                    <li>Interview-ready questions with clear answers.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Step-by-Step Comparison</h2>

                <h3 class="h5 mt-2">Step 1: Monolithic architecture (single deployable unit)</h3>
                <p>In a monolith, all modules—catalog, cart, orders, payments, and admin—live in one codebase and ship together. For a startup team, this is fast to build and easy to run in the beginning.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-3">[Web App]
 ├─ Catalog Module
 ├─ Cart Module
 ├─ Order Module
 ├─ Payment Module
 └─ Admin Module
      ↓
   One Database</pre>

                <h3 class="h5">Step 2: Layered architecture (better internal structure)</h3>
                <p>Layered architecture does not split the app into separate deployables. Instead, it structures one app into clean layers like Presentation, Business, and Data. This improves maintainability without operational complexity.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-3">Presentation Layer (MVC / API)
        ↓
Business Layer (Rules / Services)
        ↓
Data Access Layer (Repositories / EF Core)
        ↓
Database</pre>

                <h3 class="h5">Step 3: Distributed system (multiple independent services)</h3>
                <p>In a distributed system, business capabilities are split into independently deployable services. Each service can scale on its own and often owns its own data. This gives flexibility, but also introduces network, consistency, and monitoring complexity.</p>
                <pre class="diagram-box mb-0">Client → API Gateway
   ├→ Catalog Service → Catalog DB
   ├→ Order Service   → Order DB
   ├→ Payment Service → Payment DB
   └→ Notification Service
Services communicate via APIs/events</pre>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Real-World Mini Case Study (E-commerce)</h2>
                <p><strong>Phase 1:</strong> A new e-commerce startup launches with a monolith and one SQL database. Releases are quick because one team controls everything.</p>
                <p><strong>Phase 2:</strong> As features grow (discount engine, returns, seller onboarding), the team adopts a layered structure to keep business rules cleaner and reduce controller-heavy code.</p>
                <p><strong>Phase 3:</strong> During festival sales, order and payment traffic spikes dramatically. The company extracts Order and Payment into separate services, keeps Catalog in the main app temporarily, and introduces async events for order confirmation emails.</p>
                <div class="info-box mb-0">
                    This is a common migration path in industry: <strong>Monolith → Well-structured Layered Monolith → Selective Distribution</strong>. You do not need to split everything at once.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Benefits / Advantages</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolithic:</strong> simple deployment, lower early cost, faster MVP delivery.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> cleaner code organization, easier testing, better maintainability.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> independent scaling, team autonomy, fault isolation, technology flexibility.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Challenges / Limitations</h2>
                <ul class="list-tight mb-0">
                    <li><strong>Monolithic:</strong> becomes harder to scale and release as size increases.</li>
                    <li><strong>Layered:</strong> still one deployment unit, so release risk is shared.</li>
                    <li><strong>Distributed:</strong> needs strong DevOps, observability, data consistency, and failure handling.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="warning-box mt-3 mb-0">
                    Distributed systems solve scaling and autonomy problems, but they also introduce distributed complexity. Adopt them for clear business reasons, not trend pressure.
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Comparison at a Glance</h2>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table class="table table-bordered align-middle comparison-table mb-0">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Aspect</th>
                                <th>Monolithic</th>
                                <th>Layered</th>
                                <th>Distributed</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Deployment</td>
                                <td>Single unit</td>
                                <td>Single unit</td>
                                <td>Multiple independent services</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Code organization</td>
                                <td>Can become mixed over time</td>
                                <td>Structured by layers</td>
                                <td>Structured by business capability</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Scaling</td>
                                <td>Scale whole app</td>
                                <td>Scale whole app</td>
                                <td>Scale only hot services</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Operational complexity</td>
                                <td>Low</td>
                                <td>Low to medium</td>
                                <td>High</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Best fit</td>
                                <td>Early-stage products</td>
                                <td>Growing codebase in one app</td>
                                <td>Large, fast-growing platforms</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Transition Guidance: How to move safely</h2>
                <ol class="mb-0 list-tight">
                    <li>Start by cleaning boundaries inside the monolith (modules and layers).</li>
                    <li>Measure bottlenecks (slow modules, frequent release blockers, team conflicts).</li>
                    <li>Extract one high-value capability first (for example, Payments).</li>
                    <li>Introduce API contracts and event-driven integration gradually.</li>
                    <li>Invest in centralized logs, metrics, tracing, and CI/CD before large-scale migration.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Best Practices, Tips, and Common Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="row g-3">
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="good-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Best Practices</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Keep architecture decisions tied to business goals.</li>
                                <li>Prefer modular monolith before rushing to microservices.</li>
                                <li>Define clear ownership for services and data.</li>
                                <li>Design for failure: retries, timeout, and fallback strategies.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="col-lg-6">
                        <div class="bad-box h-100">
                            <h3 class="h6 fw-bold">Common Mistakes</h3>
                            <ul class="mb-0 list-tight">
                                <li>Splitting services by technical layer instead of business capability.</li>
                                <li>Creating too many tiny services too early.</li>
                                <li>Ignoring data ownership boundaries.</li>
                                <li>Migrating architecture without improving deployment automation.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Summary</h2>
                <p class="mb-0">There is no one-size-fits-all winner. Monolithic architecture is excellent for speed in early stages. Layered architecture improves quality and maintainability inside one application. Distributed systems shine when scale, release independence, and team autonomy become critical. The smart approach is to evolve intentionally, based on product maturity and measurable pain points.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="lesson-card p-4">
                <h2 class="h4 section-title">Interview Questions and Answers</h2>
                <div class="accordion" id="interviewQaAccordion">
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq1"><button class="accordion-button" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma1" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="ma1">1) What is the key difference between monolithic and distributed architecture?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma1" class="accordion-collapse collapse show" aria-labelledby="mq1" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">A monolith is deployed as one unit, while a distributed architecture is split into multiple independently deployable services that communicate over network calls or events.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq2"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma2" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma2">2) Is layered architecture the same as microservices?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma2" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq2" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">No. Layered architecture is mostly an internal organization style within one application. Microservices/distributed systems are about splitting the system into separate deployable services.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq3"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma3" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma3">3) When should a team move from monolith to distributed services?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma3" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq3" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">When clear bottlenecks appear—like scaling specific modules, slow releases due to shared code, or multiple teams frequently blocking each other.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq4"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma4" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma4">4) Why do distributed systems usually use separate databases per service?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma4" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq4" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Database-per-service enforces service ownership and loose coupling. If all services share one database, independent deployments and autonomy become difficult.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq5"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma5" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma5">5) What is one practical migration strategy interviewers like to hear?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma5" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq5" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Start with a modular monolith, identify one high-impact module (such as Payments), extract it first, and migrate incrementally with strong API contracts and observability.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq6"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma6" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma6">6) Which architecture is easiest for a small team to manage and why?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma6" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq6" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Usually monolithic or layered monolithic architecture, because there are fewer deployment pipelines, fewer network failure points, and lower operational overhead.</div></div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="accordion-item">
                        <h2 class="accordion-header" id="mq7"><button class="accordion-button collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#ma7" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="ma7">7) What is a major risk of distributed systems beyond coding complexity?</button></h2>
                        <div id="ma7" class="accordion-collapse collapse" aria-labelledby="mq7" data-bs-parent="#interviewQaAccordion"><div class="accordion-body">Operational complexity: monitoring, tracing, version compatibility, deployment coordination, and handling partial failures across services.</div></div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
